the grand Bazar in Tehran is one of the  most important social and business hubs  in Iran built centuries ago is served as  a Lifeline for many Iranians throughout  its turbulent history it has seen  several disruptions by War civil unrest  and Strikes  with the rise of e-commerce the  traditional Grand Bazaar also quickly  adopted the new business style but  nowadays shopkeepers here are struggling  as Internet disruptions push online  business to the brink of God Labs  Muhammad Raza nadav has two shops in the  bazaar three years ago he moved half of  his business online  we had a production line in our Factory  that was dedicated for e-commerce but  the Crackdown on the internet stopped  our business before that we had 30  orders per day now barely one or two our  Revenue has dropped by almost 90 percent  if this continues I'll have to go for a  layoff and some of my employees will  lose their jobs  the Iranian government has been severely  restricting internet access since the  death of 22 years old Masa amini in  police custody nearly a month ago  support widespread protest  According to some statistics 11 million  Iranians have online businesses of  Varian scales about 9 million of them  rely on Instagram Iranians are now  turned into virtual private networks or  VPN to bypass the internet disruptions  the demand for VPN Services has  increased significantly since the  protest began but the use of vpns in  tightly restricted countries comes with  its own challenges it's not difficult  for the governments to block the IP  addresses of the VPN servers as they can  be found easily and that's why in Iran  these open vpns are only available for a  short time before they are identified  and blocked by the government  Iranian officials say the internet  restriction is necessary because foreign  governments are encouraging the protest  and the country is facing an online War  provoked by its enemies outside but  inside it seems like a punishment  my clients say their pages are not  accessible Instagram and WhatsApp are  completely blocked all the sales are now  halted people who rely on e-commerce  have lost all their revenue and cannot  even pay their bills in this digital age  we're all connected when I lose Revenue  I can't buy products from others and  they cannot sell this internet blockade  has destroyed the market  it's not clear if the Iranian government  plans to continue the ban on social  platforms once the protests end however  with soaring prices inflation at 45  percent and an economy crippled by the  years of sanctions the collapse of  online businesses here will likely make  things worse Jesus Sardar Al Jazeera  Tehran
 

